00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:17.040
We tend to think that the world is organized&nbsp;
around a small group of enormous superpowers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.840
that pull the strings of everything. The United&nbsp;
States, China, the United Kingdom, maybe until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.460
recently Russia, maybe in the future, India...
 
But what if I told you that there are much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.180
smaller, seemingly insignificant&nbsp;
countries that suddenly achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.780
gigantic influence on a global scale?
 
What if we were to talk about countries that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.460
are neither the richest in the world, nor do they&nbsp;
necessarily have nuclear weapons, large armies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.200
or a large population, but have nevertheless&nbsp;
managed to carve out a niche for themselves on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.280
the international geopolitical chessboard?
 
Well, VisualPolitik viewers, in this video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.660
we are going to give you several examples of&nbsp;
countries that, despite being relatively small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.540
are breaking all the molds.
 
But first it would not be out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.620
of place to shine a light on the ultimate key to&nbsp;
its renewed influence: we are talking about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.880
is known as soft power. And, take note, this can&nbsp;
be much more important than you might imagine.
 &nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.820
We could say that this type of strategy&nbsp;
or power consists of being able to impact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.900
influence and persuade people around the&nbsp;
world to achieve certain objectives. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.860
order to do this, elements such as&nbsp;
culture, values, innovation, sports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.840
diplomacy and even philanthropy are used.
 
It is the opposite concept to hard power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.940
which is exercised through coercion, the use&nbsp;
of force or threats and economic blackmail. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.720
type of power in which, not surprisingly,&nbsp;
the great powers have many advantages.
 &nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.800
In other words, in summary, while&nbsp;
hard power depends on force, soft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:45.840
power depends on persuasion and attraction.
 
